\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Causal diagram of the group-instance model. A group of observations, $\mathbf {{\MakeUppercase []{x}}}$, is generated conditionally on a group of instance-level latent variables, $\mathbf {{\MakeUppercase []{z}}}$, and one group-level latent variable, $\MakeUppercase []{w}$.}}{28}{figure.caption.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces Probabilistic graphical model comparing the latent inference models of Information Bottleneck \citep {tishby2000information, achille2018emergence} and Domain-Content (mine). \textbf {IB:} Learning a representation $\MakeUppercase []{z}$ which is maximally predictive to the target variable $\MakeUppercase []{x}$ whilst minimizing its mutual information with the source variable $\MakeUppercase []{x}$. \citet {achille2018emergence} show that this also implies minimizing its mutual information with the nuisance variable $\MakeUppercase []{n}$. \textbf {Domain-Content:} We want the inferred domain and content variables $\MakeUppercase []{z}^q, \mathbf {{\MakeUppercase []{w}}}^q$ to be maximally informative of their data generating counterparts $\MakeUppercase []{z}, \mathbf {{\MakeUppercase []{w}}}$ and minimally informative of each other's counterpart.}}{41}{figure.caption.10}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Conditional shift in the \texttt {Shift3DIdent} dataset. It appears that the objects in the two images of the leftmost column have the same colour. However, each image was generated by a different combination of object-colour, spotlight-colour: One teapot is actually orange, while the other is bright green. We can see this by looking at other examples of the same objects under different lighting conditions. The images to the right depict different views of the same corresponding object.}}{52}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Comparison between the probabilistic graphical models of the data-generating process, the Group VAE \citep {hosoyaGroupbasedLearningDisentangled2019,bouchacourt2017multi} and our Context-Aware VAE. Our variational density conditions the content variable, $\MakeUppercase []{z}_{k}$, on the style variable, $\MakeUppercase []{w}$, in addition to the observation, $\MakeUppercase []{x}_k$.}}{55}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Our model correctly disentangles between student aptitude and school characteristics, whereas the GVAE fails. We ask the counterfactual question ``What score would student $k$ from school $A$ have obtained if they had attended the \textit {typical school}?''. The task is to generate a set of test scores by translating the scores from school $A$ onto the density of scores from the typical school. Each line shows the translation for an individual student. Our model preserves the relative positions of the scores, whilst also capturing the density of the typical school.}}{59}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Conditional shift in the dataset causes the relative performance gain of our CxVAE over the GVAE. We show performance on datasets generated with different values of the $\lambda $ hyper-parameter, controlling the amount of conditional shift. For low values of $\lambda $ the conditional density of student aptitude given a score changed very little from one group to another, and so CxVAE and GVAE perform equally well. However, as $\lambda $ increases, the GVAE fails to disentangle.}}{62}{figure.caption.23}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces The problem of \textit {versatile imputation}. The training data comprises a set of complete observation-groups, $\{y^1, \dots ,y^n\}$. The imputation task requires filling in the missing instances of an observation-group, $\mathbf {{y}}'$. We assume an underlying missingness mechanism that produces an incomplete observation-group, $\mathbf {{y}} \left [ \mathbf {{m}} \right ]'$, by combining an unseen complete observation-group, $\mathbf {{x}}'$, and a missingness pattern, $\mathbf {{m}}'$.}}{70}{figure.caption.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces The self-attention encoder. The control points of the partial input are treated as an unordered bag of instances. They are aggregated into a fixed-length vector by a self-attention mechanism. }}{74}{figure.caption.35}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Our probabilistic graphical model for the versatile imputation problem. Instance $k$ of the partial observation-group-group, $\MakeUppercase []{y} \left [ \MakeUppercase []{m} \right ]_k$, is generated by applying an instance of the missingness pattern, $\MakeUppercase []{m}_k$, to an instance of the complete observation-group-group, $\MakeUppercase []{y}_k$, with the missingness pattern. During training, only the complete observation-group-group, $\mathbf {{\MakeUppercase []{y}}}$, and the corresponding coordinates, $\mathbf {{x}}$, are observed. During testing, only the partial observation-group-group, $\mathbf {{\MakeUppercase []{y}}} \left [ \mathbf {{\MakeUppercase []{m}}} \right ]$, the coordinates, $\mathbf {{x}}$, and the missingness pattern, $\mathbf {{\MakeUppercase []{m}}}$, are observed.}}{76}{figure.caption.37}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Our MIVAE model generalises to unseen missingness densities. Shown here is the density (mean and 95-th percentiles) of the Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) on an imputation task across 7 hold-out missingness densities. The MIVAE outperforms the conventional methods Bernoulli-MAE and Pooled-MAE. MIVAE's performance comes close to the performance of the topline model Oracle-MAE, which is trained on the same missingness density used to generate the hold-out set. }}{85}{figure.caption.43}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces Our model produces plausible prosody, whereas Crude Control produces inconsistent prosody. The control points are shown with green vertical bars.}}{87}{figure.caption.48}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces Our model controls prosody efficiently. Its generated acoustic features are closer to the ground-truth rendition than is the manually modified output of the imputation model, called Crude-Control.}}{88}{figure.caption.49}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.7}{\ignorespaces 4 control points are enough to bring our model's output significantly closer to the reference utterance. These are ratings in an A/B test where the question is ``Which of these is closer to the reference ground-truth control audio?''.}}{89}{figure.caption.51}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Causal diagram for the variables involved in the diabetes risk model. A major cause of type-2 diabetes, $\MakeUppercase []{y}$, is a high body-mass index, $\MakeUppercase []{x}_{c}$, which itself is affected by whether the patient has a diet rich in salt, $W$. The frequency of thirst, $\MakeUppercase []{x}_{s}$, is symptom of both diabetes, $\MakeUppercase []{y}$, and a salt-rich diet, $\MakeUppercase []{w}$. The environments in this case correspond to different groups of patients organised by diet.}}{92}{figure.caption.53}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Causes are more consistent predictors than symptoms, although symptoms still contain important information. In (a), we can see that the probability of diabetes, $\MakeUppercase []{y}$, conditioned on a cause of diabetes (body-mass index, $\MakeUppercase []{x}_{c}$) remains constant across environments (i.e., regardless of whether salt intake, $\MakeUppercase []{w}$, is 1 or 0). However, (b) shows that the probability of diabetes, $\MakeUppercase []{y}$, conditioned on a symptom of diabetes (thirst frequency, $\MakeUppercase []{x}_{s}$) changes across environments. Nevertheless, the probability of diabetes, $\MakeUppercase []{y}$, changes with thirst frequency, $\MakeUppercase []{x}_{s}$, within each environment (i.e., when the salt intake, $\MakeUppercase []{w}$, is constant), as shown in (c).}}{94}{figure.caption.54}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces Evaluation results following the paradigm of contextual prediction. Each horizontal bar in the rugplot denotes the result for one random initialisation of the dataset and model weights. Our model, Group-Instance, which predicts jointly for an entire group of covariates, outperforms causal predictors, the current state-of-the-art under the paradigm of singleton prediction. As expected, the Naive Predictor (i.e., a model trained with maximum likelihood on the pooled data from the training environments) performs well on average but generalises poorly to new environments. The Oracle (i.e., a model similar to the Naive Predictor, but that also takes as input the ground-truth environment variable, $\MakeUppercase []{w}$, in addition to the covariates, $\MakeUppercase []{x}_{c}$ and $\MakeUppercase []{x}_{s}$) performs best on average.}}{99}{figure.caption.55}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
